On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:59:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> That's not a good fix. This lets any jail write to the package cache for
> any other jail - including the claude code user. The permissions are
> such that it shouldn't be possible, but that's still too close of a
> config.

Agreed. v1 is withdrawn. The revision is posted as "[PATCH v2] jails:
Fetch base packages into the host's cache" [1]. It mounts nothing into
the root, so the cache is never reachable from a build's chroot or a
running jail.

> I thought when using pkg from the host like that, it can use the host's
> package cache. Am I wrong about that?

Not by default, but it can be told to. I traced pkg 2.8.4 here with
truss(1). Run as "pkg -r /tmp/rt clean -n", it opens the root's
/tmp/rt/var/cache/pkg, along with the root's /var/db/pkg. With
"-o PKG_CACHEDIR=/var/cache/pkg" added it opens the host's
/var/cache/pkg as given, without the root prefix. The -C config file
does not change that. That is why your build fetched all 188 base
packages each time.

v2 does what you describe for the base install. It passes
-o PKG_CACHEDIR with the host's own setting, read by "pkg config
PKG_CACHEDIR", so only the host's pkg writes there. That covers the
163 MiB of base packages in your output.

The ports install (the 51 MiB part) is left uncached. It runs as
"pkg -c", which chroots before it looks at a cache, so the host's
cache is out of reach without a mount. The options I see:

1. Leave it uncached. It costs about 51 MiB per lab-mail build, and
   nothing is shared. I recommend this for now.

2. Install ports packages with pkg -r too, so they share the host's
   cache. I advise against it. My understanding is that pkg -r runs a
   package's shell scripts on the host, not chrooted, with
   PKG_ROOTDIR set; that is an inference from how ports write those
   scripts, which I could not confirm against the pkg source here.
   If it holds, every third-party post-install script would run as
   root on the host.

3. Fetch on the host, then copy into the root. Run "pkg -r ... fetch
   -d" against the jail's repo config into the host's cache, then copy
   those files into the root's /var/cache/pkg before pkg -c. Nothing
   is shared and the jail only writes its own copy. The costs are
   resolving the jail's repo config under -r and a copy per build.
   That is worth doing if the 51 MiB matters to you.

As before, I could not run a real build here, because lab-claude has
no zroot/jails. To check on the host, run a clean build twice. The
second should fetch no FreeBSD-* packages.

